{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanxin","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Wanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunkai","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Chunkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Siyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhu","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Xiaozhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-26T18:00:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T04:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:20:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-evolution, the ability of agents to autonomously improve their reasoning and behavior, is essential for the embodied domain with long-horizon, real-world tasks. Despite current advancements in reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) showing strong performance in enhancing reasoning in LLMs, its potential to enable self-evolving embodied intelligence with multi-modal interactions remains largely unexplored. Specifically, reinforcement fine-tuning faces two fundamental obstacles in embodied settings: (i) the lack of accessible intermediate rewards in multi-step reasoning tasks limits effective learning signals, and (ii) reliance on hand-crafted reward functions restricts generalization to novel tasks and environments. To address these challenges, we present Self-Evolving Embodied Agents-R1, SEEA-R1, the first RFT framework designed for enabling the self-evolving capabilities of embodied agents. Specifically, to convert sparse delayed rewards into denser intermediate signals that improve multi-step reasoning, we propose Tree-based group relative policy optimization (Tree-GRPO) integrates Monte Carlo Tree Search into GRPO. To generalize reward estimation across tasks and scenes, supporting autonomous adaptation and reward-driven self-evolution, we further introduce Multi-modal Generative Reward Model (MGRM). To holistically evaluate the effectiveness of SEEA-R1, we evaluate on the ALFWorld benchmark, surpassing state-of-the-art methods with scores of 85.07% (textual) and 46.27% (multi-modal), outperforming prior models including GPT-4o. SEEA-R1 also achieves scores of 80.3% (textual) and 44.03% (multi-modal) without ground truth reward, surpassing all open-source baselines and highlighting its scalability as a self-evolving embodied agent. Additional experiments and qualitative analysis further support the potential of SEEA-R1 for future research in scalable embodied intelligence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:24:08Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:24:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:58:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weimin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Weimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Vulnerable than You Think: On the Stability of Tool-Integrated LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:13:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:22:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current evaluations of tool-integrated LLM agents typically focus on end-to-end tool-usage evaluation while neglecting their stability. This limits their real-world applicability, as various internal or external factors can cause agents to crash or behave abnormally. Our research addresses this by investigating whether agents are vulnerable to errors throughout the entire tool invocation process, including reading tool documentation, selecting tools and generating parameters, and processing the tool's response. Through extensive experiments, we observe that agents are highly susceptible to errors at each stage and agents based on open-source models are more vulnerable than those based on proprietary models. We also find that increasing the model size does not significantly improve tool invocation reasoning and may make agents more vulnerable to attacks resembling normal user instructions. This highlights the importance of evaluating agent stability and offers valuable insights for future LLM development and evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21924","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21924","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21924","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong-Cheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Rong-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPAZER: Spatial-Semantic Progressive Reasoning Agent for Zero-shot 3D Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T05:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D Visual Grounding (3DVG) aims to localize target objects within a 3D scene based on natural language queries. To alleviate the reliance on costly 3D training data, recent studies have explored zero-shot 3DVG by leveraging the extensive knowledge and powerful reasoning capabilities of pre-trained LLMs and VLMs. However, existing paradigms tend to emphasize either spatial (3D-based) or semantic (2D-based) understanding, limiting their effectiveness in complex real-world applications. In this work, we introduce SPAZER - a VLM-driven agent that combines both modalities in a progressive reasoning framework. It first holistically analyzes the scene and produces a 3D rendering from the optimal viewpoint. Based on this, anchor-guided candidate screening is conducted to perform a coarse-level localization of potential objects. Furthermore, leveraging retrieved relevant 2D camera images, 3D-2D joint decision-making is efficiently performed to determine the best-matching object. By bridging spatial and semantic reasoning neural streams, SPAZER achieves robust zero-shot grounding without training on 3D-labeled data. Extensive experiments on ScanRefer and Nr3D benchmarks demonstrate that SPAZER significantly outperforms previous state-of-the-art zero-shot methods, achieving notable gains of 9.0% and 10.9% in accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21924","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:30:11Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:30:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:30:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":3,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21669%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21924%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21967%22%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}